;;; One candidate, refuted in ONE firing — M1e2 **W2** under `-L`, and its
;;; control under default flags. Register rows `W2/forced-1-L`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One object, and a rule that refutes any `(q ?x)`. Root is consistent; the
;;; blind rung's only candidate is `(q one)`, and it dies.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Root is saturated and consistent, and every remaining hypothesis makes it
;;; dead, so root is the one solution (Q-M1e.6, clause 3): **`Solution`, k = 1,
;;; a model with no `q` fact.**
;;;
;;; ── What the engine says ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   default  Solution k = 1 — the lookahead kills `(q one)` before layer 1
;;;            and caches `(not (q one))`, so `alive` is empty and root is
;;;            recorded. Right.
;;;   -L       `No solution — the constraints are contradictory`, k = 0,
;;;            `exhausted = true`, **unsat core (0 facts)**. With the
;;;            lookahead off, `alive` is the singleton `{(q one)}` and the
;;;            forced-positive cascade writes it into root as a premise-less
;;;            `<forced-positive>` fact; root is then contradictory and the run
;;;            is refuted. Promotion is valid only when root ∪ {h} is
;;;            consistent — here the right conclusion was `¬(q one)`.
;;;
;;; Banked from M1e2's probes at S1e2.1.1. The twin with the death two firings
;;; deep is `forced-2.ein`, which is wrong at default flags.
(relation thing T)
(relation q T)
(thing one)
(rule no-q () :match (q ?x) :assert (false) :priority 300)
(query :goal (thing ?x))
